- [ ] Checkpoint C visual pass (2026-09-04): please eyeball `templates/neon` and `templates/natural` overviews and example sites in both schemes. Neon: is the light half (`#3a3a42` ground, black form fields and drawer from the `color-scheme: dark` override) "less dark" enough and intentional-looking; the feature card's 4 s border sweep, the haze breathing and the `.neon-sign` flicker; the full-width feature card is very tall at desktop widths (inherited from plain). Natural: the dark soil texture strength (softened once), the pressed-specimen card's tag lift and tape at real hover speed, and whether the faint moss corner tint in light mode reads at all on your monitor.
- [ ] Checkpoint D visual pass (2026-09-04): please eyeball `templates/detailed`, `templates/gothic` and `templates/allegory` overviews and example sites in both schemes. Detailed: the dark scheme (sunburst gold on navy, body-ray density), the 92px Limelight marquee (too loud?), the stepped card's 1px gold hairline at fractional zoom. Gothic: the arched card pictures on pale stone (lancet outline may be faint), the 3.6em illuminated initial, the stained-glass wash at hover speed, ESC and light-dismiss on popovers and dialogs. Allegory: the gold and red display bands sit below 3:1 by the brief's hybrid reading (white on `#c8a44a` is 2.4:1, black on `#b3122e` is 2.9:1), the feature-card flip on hover, how the ivory pinned header reads on the dark ascent page.
- [ ] Decision 21 (2026-09-04): the three Checkpoint D templates were built to the "must not feel the same" rule; the five before it were not. Phase 11 opens with a distinctiveness pass over neumorphic, brutal-news, glassy, neon and natural. Say if any of the three new ones overshoots so that pass can be calibrated.
- [ ] Open questions still defaulted, answer before Phase 3 if you disagree: fonts by Google Fonts `<link>` (or approve a download script), Brutal News built to the TODO's words (thick rules, harsh hues) rather than the 2px reference, Allegory's white-on-gold / black-on-red kept for display bands only

## Per Template

The authority is `docs/dev/template-contract.md` (written in Phase 0 of `version-0.1.0-phased.md`); this is the summary. For each template ensure:

1. The theme is retoken compatible (https://kieranwood.ca/retoken/theme/): `css/theme.css` carries retoken's colour mapping verbatim, the 23 shared tokens with the template's own values, and the palette block in retoken's named form `:root[data-theme='<name>'], [data-theme='<name>']` with the 19 `--pal-*` slots as `light-dark()` pairs. Every page sets `<html data-theme="<name>">`. `global.css` names no colour literal. The palette block is the retoken paste-in and is printed in the README and on `docs/theme.html`.
2. It includes the following components on top of the normal form fields, buttons, typography, images, `<dialog>` modals, etc. — with the **same markup and class names in every template** (the contract), so switching templates is a stylesheet swap:
  1. Accordion (`<details class="accordion">`; `.accordion-group` shares a `name` for exclusive open)
  2. Cards (`.card` with heading, description, icon or image; `.card-horizontal` with media left, `.card-media-end` for right; `.card-feature` = one dynamic card with an effect that suits the template)
  3. Fifty-fifty (`.fifty-fifty`, image 50 %, `.fifty-fifty-reverse` for image right; stacks image-first on mobile)
  4. Image gallery with lightbox (`.gallery` + one `<dialog class="lightbox">` per gallery, `:target` slides, prev/next anchors)
  5. Toggle switch (`.switch`, `<input type="checkbox" role="switch">`)
  6. Page break (`<hr>` and the ornamented `<hr class="page-break">`)
  7. Also: buttons, `.field` form patterns, dialog, navbar, sidebar, banner, badge, table, author card, footer, and any flare the template needs (namespaced `.<name>-*`)
3. Prefer semantic html over `div`'s (`<details>`, `<dialog>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<nav>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`, `<figure>`)
4. No frameworks, no build step, no npm: pure HTML and CSS. JavaScript only from the allow-list: `js/theme.js` (scheme boot + tri-state toggle), `js/copy.js` (docs copy buttons), the landing's search, and a one-line `onclick="…showModal()"` on dialog/lightbox openers.
5. Each has a navbar layout (`index.html`, `blogpost.html`, `forms.html`, `example/`) and a sidebar layout (`sidebar.html`, `docs/`)
6. Light and dark theme via `color-scheme` + `light-dark()`; the toggle is tri-state (auto / light / dark) and uses the `retoken-scheme` key; non-colour swaps key off `<html data-scheme>`
7. A component library under `docs/components/` (index + one page per component: preview, markup with copy button, variants, classes table, notes), plus `docs/index.html` (get started), `docs/theme.html` (tokens and the palette block), `docs/defaults.html` (bare elements), `docs/blog.html` (post anatomy)
8. File structure: `templates/<name>/` with
  1. `index.html` — overview page with prose that ties the elements together and links to every other page
  2. `blogpost.html` — the fixture article (`fixtures/markdown-test.html`, verbatim) inside the post shell with an author card, tags, date, reading time
  3. `forms.html` — the fixture form (`fixtures/forms.html`, verbatim)
  4. `sidebar.html` — the sidebar-layout variant
  5. `css/theme.css`, `css/global.css`, `js/theme.js`, `js/copy.js`, `assets/` (local SVG art + favicon), optional `fonts/`
  6. `docs/` as in item 7
  7. `example/` — `index.html` + exactly two more pages that make sense for the template
  8. `README.md` — install set vs showcase set, browser floor, fonts, the retoken paste-in
9. Include it on the landing page (a card with tags from the fixed vocabulary) and as a row in `templates/README.md`; previews are hand-drawn SVGs in `landing/previews/`
10. Passes `python tools/check.py --template <name>` and a visual pass in both schemes

## Per change
Required guidence to follow per change

- Check off changes as you go
  - Make sure the TODO is kept up to date so that if a usage limit is hit I'm not left in the dark
- Add Changes to the CHANGELOG.md
    - If a version exists and does not have a release date yet, keep adding to it, if there's no versions without a release date, then bump version (e.g. `v0.1.0 -> v0.2.0`) in the version file, and add a new main heading to CHANGELOG.md for it (e.g. `v0.2.0 (unreleased)`) and add the changes under there.
    - Be brief, 2-4 setnences per change, and if you need more, prompt for approval from the user
    - Add all changes under the following categories as sub-headings:
      - Features
      - Bug Fixes
      - Other
        - For things like
          - Documentation improvements
          - Refinements to existing systems that weren't bugs
          - anything else that doesn't fit in the other sections